  "title": "Silent model drift: catch it before prod",
  "content": "Opinions on evaluation gap tend to change fast after watching it fail somewhere it wasn't supposed to. It's a one-line fix once you know where to look, and expensive until then. This covers the fix, the cost of not knowing sooner, and what to monitor.\n\n## The error and why it's confusing\n\nYou deploy a new model version. All dashboards are green: latency normal, error rate flat, cost stable. Then a week later, support tickets trickle in — not a flood, just a steady drip. \"The assistant used to be polite, now it's curt.\" \"It stopped asking for confirmation before deleting.\" \"It's recommending products we discontinued.\" You dig into logs and find nothing obviously broken. The model isn't crashing; it's just *different* in a way that no metric caught.\n\nThis is the evaluation gap: the space between what your monitoring measures and what your users actually experience. Traditional metrics — accuracy, F1, BLEU, even LLM-as-judge scores — are often too coarse or too narrow to detect subtle behavioral shifts. A model can maintain 92% accuracy on your test set while becoming 30% more likely to hallucinate a specific edge case, or 50% less likely to follow a formatting instruction. If your evaluation suite doesn't cover that behavior, you won't see it until users complain.\n\nThe confusing part is that *nothing looks wrong*. Your CI pipeline passed. Your canary deployment showed no regression. Your A/B test — if you even ran one — focused on a primary metric like click-through or task completion, which may not be sensitive to the drift. The model is technically \"working,\" just not the way it used to. And because the change is gradual or context-dependent, it's easy to dismiss early signals as noise.\n\nThis post is about closing that gap. It's written for teams running LLM-powered features in production — especially in Southeast Asia, where lean teams often skip heavy evaluation infrastructure to ship faster. The goal is to give you a practical troubleshooting framework: how to detect silent model drift, how to diagnose its cause, and how to prevent it from slipping through again.\n\n## What's actually causing it (the real reason, not the surface symptom)\n\nThe root cause is almost never a single bug. It's a mismatch between *what you evaluate* and *what matters*. Here are the common mechanisms:\n\n1. **Evaluation set staleness.** Your test set was built six months ago. Since then, user behavior shifted — new slang, new product names, new edge cases. The model may still ace the old tests while failing on new inputs that were never represented.\n\n2. **Metric myopia.** You're optimizing for a proxy (e.g., perplexity, BLEU, or a single LLM-judge score) that doesn't correlate with the behaviors users care about: tone, safety, instruction adherence, factual consistency on domain-specific facts.\n\n3. **Distribution shift in inputs.** Your users start asking different questions — maybe because of a marketing campaign, a new feature, or a seasonal event. The model wasn't retrained, but the input distribution changed, exposing weaknesses that were always there but rarely triggered.\n\n4. **Model provider updates.** If you're using a hosted LLM API, the provider may silently update the model behind the same endpoint. This is common: many providers reserve the right to improve models without version bumps. Your code didn't change, but the model did.\n\n5. **Prompt or pipeline drift.** A seemingly innocuous change — reordering few-shot examples, tweaking a system prompt, updating a retrieval index — can alter model behavior in subtle ways. These changes often bypass rigorous evaluation because they're considered \"minor.\"\n\n6. **Feedback loops.** If your system learns from user interactions (e.g., fine-tuning on logged conversations), it can drift toward whatever patterns are most frequent, even if those patterns are undesirable (e.g., terse responses because most users don't complain about brevity).\n\nThe surface symptom — \"the model got worse\" — is usually a lagging indicator. By the time users notice, the drift has been present for days or weeks. The real problem is that you lacked a *behavioral* evaluation layer that could catch it earlier.\n\n## Fix 1 — the most common cause: stale evaluation sets\n\n**Symptom pattern:** Your offline evaluation metrics are stable or improving, but user complaints are rising. The complaints cluster around specific scenarios — e.g., handling refund requests, explaining technical concepts, or responding to angry customers. When you manually test those scenarios, the model often fails, but your automated tests pass.\n\n**Diagnosis:** Your evaluation set doesn't cover the failing scenarios. This is the most common cause of the evaluation gap. Test sets are often built once and never refreshed, while real-world usage evolves.\n\n**Fix:** Implement a process to continuously curate evaluation data from production traffic. This doesn't mean labeling everything — it means sampling strategically.\n\nHere's a minimal, runnable Python snippet that samples recent production logs and flags potential evaluation candidates using a simple heuristic: low confidence or user negative feedback.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport random\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\n# Assume logs are stored as JSON lines with fields:\n# timestamp, user_input, model_output, confidence_score, user_feedback\n\ndef sample_evaluation_candidates(log_path, days=7, sample_size=100):\n    cutoff = datetime.utcnow() - timedelta(days=days)\n    candidates = []\n    with open(log_path, 'r') as f:\n        for line in f:\n            entry = json.loads(line)\n            ts = datetime.fromisoformat(entry['timestamp'])\n            if ts < cutoff:\n                continue\n            # Heuristic: low confidence or explicit negative feedback\n            if entry.get('confidence_score', 1.0) < 0.7 or entry.get('user_feedback') == 'negative':\n                candidates.append(entry)\n    # Random sample to keep labeling effort manageable\n    if len(candidates) > sample_size:\n        candidates = random.sample(candidates, sample_size)\n    return candidates\n\n# Usage: run weekly, send candidates to labeling queue\n# candidates = sample_evaluation_candidates('prod_logs.jsonl')\n```\n\nThis is illustrative — adjust thresholds and fields to match your logging schema. The key is to create a feedback loop: production data → evaluation set → model improvement → production. Without it, your evaluation set becomes a museum piece.\n\n**Action:** Set up a weekly job to sample 50–100 recent interactions, especially those with negative feedback or low confidence. Have a human label them for correctness, tone, and instruction adherence. Add them to your evaluation suite. This alone can catch many drifts.\n\n## Fix 2 — the less obvious cause: metric myopia\n\n**Symptom pattern:** Your primary metric (e.g., accuracy, BLEU, or an LLM-judge score) is flat, but users report specific behavioral changes: the model is more verbose, less empathetic, or ignores certain instructions. When you look at aggregate scores, nothing stands out.\n\n**Diagnosis:** You're measuring the wrong thing. Aggregate metrics average away the behaviors that matter. A model can maintain high overall accuracy while becoming significantly worse on a critical subset — say, medical dosage questions or financial advice disclaimers.\n\n**Fix:** Move from a single scalar metric to a *behavioral checklist*. Define the specific behaviors your application requires, and evaluate each independently. For example:\n\n- **Instruction adherence:** Does the model follow formatting rules (e.g., \"always respond in JSON\")?\n- **Tone:** Is the response polite, professional, and aligned with brand voice?\n- **Safety:** Does it refuse harmful requests without over-refusing benign ones?\n- **Factual consistency:** Does it avoid contradicting known facts or previous turns?\n- **Conciseness:** Does it respect length constraints?\n\nYou can implement this with a combination of rule-based checks and targeted LLM judges. Here's a simple example using a hypothetical judge prompt (you'd use your own model):\n\n```python\n# Pseudocode for a behavioral check using an LLM judge\n# In practice, call your LLM API with a structured prompt\n\ndef check_instruction_adherence(response, instruction):\n    judge_prompt = f\"\"\"\n    Instruction: {instruction}\n    Response: {response}\n    Does the response strictly follow the instruction? Answer YES or NO.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Call your LLM here; return True if YES\n    # This is a placeholder for the actual API call\n    return True  # Replace with real logic\n\n# Example usage\ninstruction = \"Respond in JSON with keys 'answer' and 'confidence'.\"\nresponse = '{\"answer\": \"yes\", \"confidence\": 0.9}'\nassert check_instruction_adherence(response, instruction) == True\n```\n\nThis is illustrative; the judge model should be different from the one being evaluated to avoid self-bias. The point is to create a suite of binary or graded checks that map directly to user expectations. Run these on every model change — including provider-side updates.\n\n**Action:** Write down 5–10 specific behaviors your model must exhibit. For each, create a small test set (20–50 examples) and a clear pass/fail criterion. Automate this as part of your CI/CD pipeline. If a new model version fails any check, block the deploy.\n\n## Fix 3 — the environment-specific cause: silent provider updates\n\n**Symptom pattern:** You haven't changed your code, prompts, or data, but the model's behavior shifted overnight. The change is consistent across all users. You check your deployment logs and see no new releases. If you're using a hosted LLM API, this is a strong signal.\n\n**Diagnosis:** Many LLM providers update their models without changing the endpoint name. They might release a new version under the same alias (e.g., `gpt-4` becomes `gpt-4-0613` then `gpt-4-1106-preview`). If you're not pinning to a specific version, you're at the mercy of their release schedule.\n\n**Fix:** Pin your model versions explicitly. Most providers offer versioned endpoints (e.g., `gpt-4-0613` instead of `gpt-4`). Use them. Then, when a new version is released, you can test it in staging before rolling it out.\n\nAdditionally, implement a canary deployment for model changes. Route a small percentage of traffic to the new version, and compare behavioral metrics against the baseline. If the new version fails any check, roll back.\n\nHere's a simplified canary logic in Python:\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef route_request(user_id, new_model_version, canary_percent=5):\n    # Deterministic routing based on user_id to avoid flapping\n    if hash(user_id) % 100 < canary_percent:\n        return new_model_version\n    return 'stable-model-version'\n\n# Usage: in your request handler\n# model = route_request(user_id, 'new-model-2026-01')\n```\n\nThis is illustrative; in production, you'd use a feature flag system. The key is to isolate the new version and monitor its behavior closely.\n\n**Action:** Check your LLM provider's documentation for version pinning. If they don't offer it, consider using a gateway that lets you route to specific model versions. Then, set up a canary deployment for any model change — even those you don't initiate.\n\n## How to verify the fix worked\n\nAfter implementing these fixes, you need to verify that you've actually closed the evaluation gap. Here's a practical verification protocol:\n\n1. **Re-run your new behavioral checks on the current production model.** They should pass. If they fail, you've found a drift that was already present — good, now you can fix it.\n\n2. **Simulate a drift.** Take an older model version (or a deliberately degraded one) and run it through your checks. They should fail. This confirms your checks are sensitive enough.\n\n3. **Monitor the rate of user complaints.** After deploying the fixes, track the number of behavioral complaints per 1,000 requests. It should drop. If it doesn't, your checks may not be covering the right behaviors.\n\n4. **Set up alerts.** Define thresholds for your behavioral metrics. For example, if instruction adherence drops below 95%, trigger an alert. This gives you early warning.\n\n5. **Document the gap.** Write a postmortem-style note (even if it's just for your team) explaining what slipped through and why. This helps refine your evaluation strategy.\n\n## How to prevent this from happening again\n\nPrevention is about building a culture of continuous evaluation. Here are the key practices:\n\n- **Treat evaluation as code.** Store your test sets, judges, and thresholds in version control. Run them automatically on every model change (including provider updates).\n\n- **Diversify your metrics.** Don't rely on a single score. Use a mix of automated checks, human review, and user feedback.\n\n- **Close the loop with production.** Regularly sample production data and add edge cases to your evaluation set. This is the single most effective way to catch drift.\n\n- **Pin versions and canary.** Never let a model change go straight to 100% of traffic without a canary phase.\n\n- **Assign ownership.** Someone on the team should own model quality. It shouldn't be a side responsibility.\n\n## Related errors you might hit next\n\n- **\"Model output truncated unexpectedly\"** — often caused by a change in max token limits or a provider-side update.\n- **\"Rate limit exceeded\"** — can happen if a new model version has different rate limits, or if your retry logic amplifies load.\n- **\"Invalid API key\"** — sometimes occurs when a provider rotates keys or deprecates an old version.\n- **\"Context length exceeded\"** — if a model update reduces the context window, your long prompts may start failing.\n- **\"Unexpected JSON response\"** — if the model becomes more verbose or changes formatting, your parser may break.\n\n## When none of these work: escalation path\n\nIf you've tried the above and still see unexplained behavior changes, consider these steps:\n\n1. **Reproduce with a minimal example.** Strip away your application logic and test the model directly with the same input. If the behavior persists, it's likely provider-side.\n\n2. **Contact your provider.** If you have a support contract, open a ticket. Provide specific examples and timestamps. Ask if there have been any recent updates.\n\n3. **Check community forums.** Other teams may be seeing the same issue. Look for discussions on platforms like GitHub, Reddit, or the provider's own forum.\n\n4. **Consider switching providers.** If the provider is unresponsive and the drift is unacceptable, evaluate alternatives. This is a last resort, but sometimes necessary.\n\n5. **Build a fallback.** If you can't control the model, build a fallback mechanism — e.g., a rule-based system that handles critical cases.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How often should I refresh my evaluation set?**\n\nAt minimum, monthly. But if your application is user-facing and evolving quickly, weekly sampling is better. The goal is to keep the evaluation set representative of current traffic. A good rule of thumb: if your production data distribution has shifted significantly (new intents, new entities), refresh immediately.\n\n**Can I use LLM-as-judge for behavioral checks?**\n\nYes, but with caveats. LLM judges are convenient but can be biased toward the model being evaluated (especially if it's the same family). Use a different model for judging, and always validate the judge against human labels on a small set. For critical behaviors (safety, compliance), prefer rule-based checks or human review.\n\n**What's the difference between model drift and concept drift?**\n\nModel drift refers to changes in the model's behavior over time, often due to provider updates or fine-tuning. Concept drift refers to changes in the relationship between inputs and outputs in the real world — e.g., what constitutes a \"good\" answer changes because user expectations or facts change. Both can cause the evaluation gap, but they require different mitigations: model drift is about version control and testing; concept drift is about updating your evaluation criteria.\n\n**How do I convince my team to invest in evaluation infrastructure?**\n\nStart small. Implement a lightweight behavioral check for one critical feature. Show how it caught a subtle regression that would have otherwise gone unnoticed. Quantify the cost of not catching it (e.g., support tickets, churn). Use that to argue for more resources. Remember: in lean teams, the biggest risk is shipping a broken experience to millions of users — evaluation is insurance.\n\n## Your next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your production logging system and query for the last 100 user interactions where feedback was negative or confidence was low. Manually review 10 of them. Look for patterns: are there specific scenarios where the model consistently underperforms? Write down one behavior you can turn into a test case. That's your first behavioral check. Add it to your CI pipeline today.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
